\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Main hardware components of a neutral atom QPU. The trapping laser light (red) is shaped by a Spatial Light Modulator (SLM). Moving tweezers (purple), used to rearrange atoms, are controlled by a 2D Acousto-Optic Deflector (AOD) and superimposed using a Polarizing Beam-Splitter (PBS). The green fluorescence emitted by the atoms is separated by a dichroic mirror and captured by a camera during the readout phase. Image adapted from \cite  {Henriet2020quantumcomputing}.}}{8}{figure.1.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:qpu_components}{{1.2}{8}{Main hardware components of a neutral atom QPU. The trapping laser light (red) is shaped by a Spatial Light Modulator (SLM). Moving tweezers (purple), used to rearrange atoms, are controlled by a 2D Acousto-Optic Deflector (AOD) and superimposed using a Polarizing Beam-Splitter (PBS). The green fluorescence emitted by the atoms is separated by a dichroic mirror and captured by a camera during the readout phase. Image adapted from \cite {Henriet2020quantumcomputing}}{figure.1.2}{}}
